Spill regulation in resonant extraction depends on the beam response to transverse excitation and on the time required for unstable particles to reach the septum. We study these effects for the Fermilab Mu2e Delivery Ring using Synergia2 tracking and a reduced Kobayashi-Hamiltonian model, with MARS transport to examine the subsequent motion of septum-scattered particles. Fixed-tune tests of radio-frequency knockout (RFKO) excitation show greater emittance growth and smaller centroid excursions at nonzero chromaticity, consistent with chromatic phase mixing. In an extraction case with imposed quadrupole ripple, a proportional--derivative RFKO controller reduces the visible ripple-driven spill modulation, with residual spikes. Tune-step diagnostics at three extraction stages show transit times from a few to more than one hundred turns, increasing as the starting horizontal coordinate lies farther from the septum in the displayed samples; the reduced model reproduces these trends but differs quantitatively from full tracking. In the stated \SI{50}{\micro\metre}-foil scattering model, approximately 43\% of the scattered-particle sample returned from MARS is lost during subsequent ring tracking; this fraction is conditional on that sample and is not a total extraction-loss estimate. These results extend the published feed-forward study by identifying particle-response effects relevant to RFKO regulation and by following transport beyond septum interception. They are configuration-specific and do not quantify the performance of a complete spill-control system.
